    "response": " Let's denote the radius of the cylinder by \\( r \\) and the height by \\( h \\). The volume of the cylinder is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\nWe are given that the volume is 500 cm^3, so we have the constraint:\n\n\\[ \\pi r^2 h = 500 \\]\n\nThe cost of the metal for the top and bottom is twice as much per cm^2 as the metal for the side. The surface area of the top and bottom is \\( 2\\pi r^2 \\) and the surface area of the side is \\( 2\\pi rh \\). Therefore, the total cost \\( C \\) can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ C = 2 \\cdot 2\\pi r^2 + 1 \\cdot 2\\pi rh = 4\\pi r^2 + 2\\pi rh \\]\n\nWe need to minimize this cost subject to the constraint \\( \\pi r^2 h = 500 \\). To use the method of Lagrange multipliers, we introduce a Lagrange multiplier \\( \\lambda \\) and consider the Lagrangian function:\n\n\\[ \\mathcal{L}(r, h, \\lambda) = 4\\pi r^2 + 2\\pi rh + \\lambda (500 - \\pi r^2 h) \\]\n\nWe take the partial derivatives of \\( \\mathcal{L} \\) with respect to \\( r \\), \\( h \\), and \\( \\lambda \\) and set them equal to zero:\n\n\\[ \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial r} = 8\\pi r + 2\\pi h - \\lambda \\cdot 2\\pi rh = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial h} = 2\\pi r - \\lambda \\cdot \\pi r^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{\\partial \\mathcal{L}}{\\partial \\lambda} = 500 - \\pi r^2 h = 0 \\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we can solve for \\( \\lambda \\):\n\n\\[ 2\\pi r = \\lambda \\cdot \\pi r^2 \\]\n\\[ \\lambda = \\frac{2}{r} \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( \\lambda = \\frac{2}{r} \\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[ 8\\pi r + 2\\pi h - \\frac{2}{r} \\cdot 2\\pi rh = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8\\pi r + 2\\pi h - 4\\pi h = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8\\pi r - 2\\pi h = 0 \\]\n\\[ 4r = h \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( h = 4r \\) into the constraint equation:\n\n\\[ \\pi r^2 (4r) = 500 \\]\n\\[ 4\\pi r^3 = 500 \\]\n\\[ r^3 = \\frac{500}{4\\pi} \\]\n\\[ r^3 = \\frac{125}{\\pi} \\]\n\\[ r = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{\\pi}} \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi}} \\]\n\nSince \\( h = 4r \\), we have:\n\n\\[ h = 4 \\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi}} = \\frac{20}{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi}} \\]\n\nTherefore, the radius and height that minimize the cost are:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\left( \\frac{5}{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi}}, \\frac{20}{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi}} \\right)} \\]",
